</svg>
  </Svg>

  <FlashCard id="fc-fi-curve">
    <Front>What does the f-I curve represent?</Front>
    <Back>The relationship between input current strength and firing rate (spikes per second). It shows how neurons encode stimulus intensity into spike frequency—a key mechanism of rate coding.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Time-Varying Inputs and Computational Considerations</H2>
    <Body>
When input varies in time, $I = I(t)$, the IF model's behavior becomes richer—and we lose analytical tractability. Now spike timing depends on:
- **Input magnitude**: stronger bursts trigger spikes faster
- **Input duration**: brief pulses may not bring voltage to threshold
- **Input timing**: when a pulse arrives relative to the last spike matters (membrane integrates recent history)

This is where numerical simulation becomes essential. Key implementation tips:

**1. Time step selection**: Choose $\Delta t \ll \tau$ (rule of thumb: $\Delta t \leq \tau/10$) to avoid numerical instability.

**2. Threshold overshoot**: Euler integration can make $V$ jump *past* threshold. After reset, check if you need interpolation for precise spike times (for timing-critical applications).

**3. Computational efficiency**: IF models are cheap to simulate (one ODE, one threshold check per step). You can easily run networks of thousands of neurons.

**4. Stochastic input**: Adding noise to $I(t)$ (from synaptic fluctuations) makes spike trains irregular, matching biology better. We'll cover this in stochastic neuron models.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <Section type="tip">
    <H2>Model Limitations: What IF Models Miss</H2>
    <Body>
The IF model's simplicity is its strength *and* weakness. Here's what it deliberately ignores:

**No action potential shape**: The model emits delta-function spikes, not the ~1ms waveforms real neurons produce. For network-level questions, this is fine. For understanding ion channel biophysics, it's useless.

**No refractoriness**: Real neurons have an absolute refractory period (~1-2ms) where they *cannot* fire, and a relative refractory period where threshold is elevated. IF models can fire arbitrarily fast if input is strong enough—biologically unrealistic.

**No spike-frequency adaptation**: Many neurons slow their firing during sustained input (K⁺ channel activation, synaptic depression). Basic IF is memoryless between spikes.

**No subthreshold resonance**: Some neurons prefer oscillatory inputs at specific frequencies due to active currents (Ih, IM). IF models have flat frequency response.

**Solutions**: Extended models add these features—refractory IF, adaptive IF (AdEx), exponential IF. But the core integrate-and-fire principle remains: charge accumulation + threshold.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-if-limitations">
    <Front>What are two major biological features the basic IF model omits?</Front>
    <Back>1) Action potential shape (uses delta spikes), 2) Refractory period (can fire arbitrarily fast). Also missing: spike-frequency adaptation, subthreshold resonance, and detailed voltage-gated dynamics.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Summary: Why IF Models Endure</H2>
    <Body>
Over a century after its introduction, the integrate-and-fire model remains the workhorse of computational neuroscience. Why?

**Mathematical tractability**: Closed-form solutions for steady-state, analytical firing rates, and tractable network dynamics.

**Computational efficiency**: Minimal state variables (just $V$) and simple threshold logic make large-scale simulations feasible.

**Conceptual clarity**: The model isolates the *essence* of spiking—integration vs. threshold—without the clutter of dozens of ion channels.

**Extensibility**: Easy to add features incrementally (refractoriness, adaptation, noise) while keeping the core framework.

You've now mastered:
- The RC circuit foundation and membrane time constant
- Threshold and reset mechanisms that create discrete spikes
- Analytical solutions for constant input and firing rate derivation
- Numerical implementation with proper threshold handling
- The f-I curve and its biological interpretation
- Model limitations and when to use more complex alternatives

Next, we'll extend this to networks of IF neurons and explore how collective dynamics emerge from coupled threshold oscillators.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <SingleSelect id="q1-rc-equation">
    <Prompt>In the RC circuit equation for the IF model, $C \frac{dV}{dt} = -g_L(V - V_{\text{rest}}) + I(t)$, what does the term $-g_L(V - V_{\text{rest}})$ represent?</Prompt>
    <Options>
      <Option correct="true">The leak current that pulls voltage back toward resting potential</Option>
      <Option>The capacitive charging current from external input</Option>
      <Option>The threshold-crossing mechanism that triggers spikes</Option>
      <Option>The synaptic conductance change during activation</Option>
    </Options>
  </SingleSelect>

  <SingleSelect id="q2-membrane-tau">
    <Prompt>A neuron has capacitance $C = 100$ pF and leak conductance $g_L = 10$ nS. What is its membrane time constant $\tau$?</Prompt>
    <Options>
      <Option>1 ms</Option>
      <Option correct="true">10 ms</Option>
      <Option>100 ms</Option>
      <Option>1000 ms</Option>
    </Options>
  </SingleSelect>

  <SingleSelect id="q3-rheobase">
    <Prompt>For an IF neuron with $V_{\text{rest}} = -70$ mV, $V_{\text{thre}} = -50$ mV, and $g_L = 10$ nS, what is the rheobase current $I_{\text{rheo}}$?</Prompt>
    <Options>
      <Option>20 pA</Option>
      <Option>50 pA</Option>
      <Option correct="true">200 pA</Option>
      <Option>500 pA</Option>
    </Options>
  </SingleSelect>

  <MultiSelect id="q4-if-properties">
    <Prompt>Which of the following statements about the integrate-and-fire model are true? (Select all that apply)</Prompt>
    <Options>
      <Option correct="true">It combines continuous subthreshold dynamics with discrete threshold-crossing events</Option>
      <Option correct="true">For constant suprathreshold input, it produces periodic spiking with constant ISI</Option>
      <Option>It accurately reproduces the detailed waveform of biological action potentials</Option>
      <Option correct="true">Below rheobase current, voltage asymptotically approaches a subthreshold steady state</Option>
      <Option>It includes a biologically realistic refractory period after each spike</Option>
    </Options>
  </MultiSelect>

  <MultiSelect id="q5-fi-curve">
    <Prompt>Which features characterize the f-I curve of the basic integrate-and-fire model? (Select all that apply)</Prompt>
    <Options>
      <Option correct="true">Firing rate is zero for input below rheobase</Option>
      <Option correct="true">There is a discontinuous jump in firing rate at the rheobase threshold</Option>
      <Option>Firing rate grows linearly with input for all suprathreshold currents</Option>
      <Option correct="true">At very high input, firing rate saturates near $1/\tau$</Option>
      <Option>The curve shows spike-frequency adaptation with sustained input</Option>
    </Options>
  </MultiSelect>

  <SortQuiz id="q6-if-simulation-steps">
    <Prompt>Arrange the following steps in the correct order for numerically simulating an IF neuron using Euler's method:</Prompt>
    <SortedItems>
      <Item>Initialize voltage to reset value $V_{\text{reset}}$</Item>
      <Item>Compute $dV/dt$ using the RC circuit equation</Item>
      <Item>Update voltage: $V_{n+1} = V_n + (dV/dt) \cdot \Delta t$</Item>
      <Item>Check if $V_{n+1} \geq V_{\text{thre}}$</Item>
      <Item>If threshold crossed, record spike time and reset $V$ to $V_{\text{reset}}$</Item>
      <Item>Continue to next time step</Item>
    </SortedItems>
  </SortQuiz>

  <MatchPairs id="q7-if-parameters">
    <Prompt>Match each IF model parameter to its biological or computational meaning:</Prompt>
    <Pairs>
      <Pair>
        <Left>$\tau = C/g_L$</Left>
        <Right>Membrane time constant (response timescale)</Right>
      </Pair>
      <Pair>
        <Left>$V_{\text{thre}}$</Left>
        <Right>Spike threshold potential</Right>
      </Pair>
      <Pair>
        <Left>$I_{\text{rheo}}$</Left>
