  <Subjective id="q9-if-limitations">
    <Prompt>Explain why the basic integrate-and-fire model cannot capture spike-frequency adaptation, and describe what biological mechanism causes this phenomenon in real neurons. How would you extend the IF model to include adaptation? (50-150 words)</Prompt>
    <Rubric>
      <Criterion points="3" required="true">
        <Requirement>Explains that basic IF resets instantaneously with no memory between spikes, making it memoryless</Requirement>
        <Indicators>memoryless, instantaneous reset, no history, stateless between spikes</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
      <Criterion points="3" required="true">
        <Requirement>Identifies biological mechanism: calcium-activated potassium channels, sodium channel inactivation, or synaptic depression</Requirement>
        <Indicators>calcium, potassium, K+, Ca2+, channel inactivation, synaptic depression</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
      <Criterion points="2">
        <Requirement>Proposes extension: add adaptation current or dynamic threshold that accumulates with spikes</Requirement>
        <Indicators>adaptation current, dynamic threshold, adaptive, accumulate, spike-dependent</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
      <Criterion points="2">
        <Requirement>Mentions that adaptation causes firing rate to decrease during sustained input</Requirement>
        <Indicators>decrease, slow down, reduce firing, sustained input, prolonged stimulation</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
    </Rubric>
    <Constraints minWords="50" maxWords="150" />
  </Subjective>

  <Subjective id="q10-fi-curve-biology">
    <Prompt>Why does the f-I curve of the basic IF model have a discontinuous jump at rheobase, and how does this compare to real neuronal f-I curves? What does this tell us about the model's assumptions? (50-150 words)</Prompt>
    <Rubric>
      <Criterion points="4" required="true">
        <Requirement>Explains that at rheobase, voltage exactly reaches threshold after infinite time, causing mathematical discontinuity from zero to finite firing rate</Requirement>
        <Indicators>infinite time, asymptotically, mathematical discontinuity, exactly reaches, I=I_rheo</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
      <Criterion points="3">
        <Requirement>Notes that real neurons have smoother f-I curves due to noise, channel stochasticity, or gradual threshold approach</Requirement>
        <Indicators>smoother, gradual, noise, stochastic, real neurons, biological</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
      <Criterion points="3">
        <Requirement>Discusses assumption: model assumes deterministic threshold and instantaneous reset with no variability</Requirement>
        <Indicators>deterministic, instantaneous, no variability, sharp threshold, assumption</Indicators>
      </Criterion>
    </Rubric>
    <Constraints minWords="50" maxWords="150" />
  </Subjective>

